WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.640
If this hourglass is your money, the top

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:06.320
is your bank account, and every grain

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.120
falling to the bottom is leaking into a

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.960
trap you never [music] agreed to. That's

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.880
the scary part, like you probably have

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.080
no idea it's happening because money

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.280
problems don't feel dramatic at first.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.480
The tap to pay, the car payment, the $28

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.080
DoorDash delivery, [music] they all feel

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.600
normal. But then, when you check your

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.720
account after what seems like a decent

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.440
paycheck, and it's still draining away,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.280
that's something else. In fact, there

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.785
are five traps draining your bank

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.640
account right now. And also draining

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:34.800
[music] your business if you have one.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.720
You need to stop every one of those. So,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.300
I'm going to walk you through them one

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.400
at a time, showing you exactly how each

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.760
[music] one is draining you, and I'm

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.200
going to hand you the way to flip it.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.960
And this last one, if you don't fix it,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.640
will drain over a hundred thousand

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:53.520
dollars over the course of your life.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.480
Trap one, payments over price. The first

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.440
leak starts when a seller gets you to

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.480
stop asking, "What does this cost?" and

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.760
instead starts asking, "Can I handle

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.760
that payment?" That tiny language,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.280
that's a really expensive shift. A

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.320
$45,000 car becomes only 745 bucks a

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.440
month. A couch even becomes $58 a month,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.680
which is crazy now. And a phone becomes

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.520
$0 down. But let's look at this

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.600
hourglass for a second. The trap works

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.720
because the pain arrives in these tiny

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.600
little small doses. And there's actually

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.080
a bunch of research on this. There's

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.160
this thing called the pain of pain. So,

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.045
when payment feels distant, delayed, or

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.240
split apart from the big purchase,

00:01:31.045 --> 00:01:33.760
[music] you actually feel less pain in

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.800
the moment. So, you'll spend spend spend

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.240
more easily. Experian actually found

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.160
that the average new car payment is 745

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.720
a month. What is that? [music] That's

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.240
8,940 bucks a year before insurance,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.080
gas, maintenance, registration, and you

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.480
know, the small spiritual tax [music] of

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.600
pretending the car was a financial

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.520
decision that makes you money.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.160
And that's why these payments are a trap

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.200
because [music] they make you feel smart

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.400
for affording a slice while the seller

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.360
quietly sells you the whole pie. You're

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.479
going to stop it with what we call the

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.000
three-number test. Number one, the

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.800
sticker price. That's the real cost, not

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.560
the monthly. I want you to write it down

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.840
before anything else because every trick

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.160
from [music] here on basically exists to

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.800
make you forget this number. Then we're

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.080
going to go with number two, that's the

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.440
total interest. This is what you pay for

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.200
the privilege of not paying immediately.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.560
The math is pretty simple. Monthly

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.520
payment times number of months minus

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.520
sticker price. That equals your total

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.320
interest. So when you subtract out the

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.360
45,000 sticker, [music]

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.520
you've just handed the lender 8,000-plus

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.000
dollars for the favor of giving you more

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.280
time. And that's like the leak that

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.840
you're never going to see on the window

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.840
sticker.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.360
And also why they always want you to

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.880
finance everything. Number three, the

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.080
opportunity cost. This one's really hard

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.301
because

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.760
it's the money the purchase quietly

00:02:50.301 --> 00:02:54.200
[music] costs your future self. Let's

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.196
say you like take the sticker price and

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.680
ask, "What if it becomes something you

00:02:56.196 --> 00:02:59.920
[music] had invested in instead?" The

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.680
formula for that is what's called future

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.680
value. This is maybe the only useful

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.680
thing you'll learn in college from

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.080
economics. [music]

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.320
You basically take the amount times 1

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.519
year [music] plus the return multiplied

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.882
by the number of years. And so if you're

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:15.680
thinking about like a 7% return, which

00:03:13.882 --> 00:03:18.959
[music] would be very reasonable in the

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.040
stock market, that 45K turns into

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:25.239
roughly in 10 years about 88,000

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:28.680
dollars. In 20 years, about 174,000

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:30.320
dollars. In 30 years, about 342K.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.360
So the real price of the car isn't

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.120
45,000, it's 45,000 plus the interest

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.440
plus 342,000 dollars over the course of

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.600
the car. And that's where the monthly

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.959
payment is kind of hiding in here. And

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.640
this doesn't just apply to your personal

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:45.120
finances, it happens all the time with

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.680
business owners that I see in our

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.000
Contrarian Thinking communities. An

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.519
overpriced vendor gives them a favor by

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.280
giving them a payment plan. They need

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:54.160
money quickly and accept a high-interest

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.600
loan without about the payback. So, I

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.960
want you to run those three numbers

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.800
before any payment plan. If the full

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.360
number sort of embarrasses you, the

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.440
monthly number is hiding something.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.972
>> I am very, very sneaky, sir.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.800
>> Trap number two, convenience disguised

00:04:04.972 --> 00:04:08.520
[music] as necessity. This one feels

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.080
innocent. It's like the ride you booked

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:13.280
because walking just was too [music] hot

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.040
that day. Or the same-day shipping you

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.600
paid extra for on something you didn't

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.600
need today. The coffee that you didn't

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.519
plan for. We call this the convenience

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.400
fee. And it's the privilege of giving

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.160
someone your money [music] so that you

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.040
get something quickly. And the little

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.600
lie at checkout is often, it's just this

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.000
one time. And sometimes it is. [music]

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.520
But convenience is rarely a one-time

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.600
thing. Convenience becomes a trap when

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.120
you buy it at your weakest moment.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.680
Anytime you swipe your credit card when

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.840
you're tired, late, [music] stressed, or

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.520
overstimulated, your brain is not

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.400
actually thinking. You're not running a

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.640
personal finance seminar at 8:47 p.m.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.760
when you're watching [music]

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.320
a TV show and you had a really hard day

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.080
at work and you just like want one

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.520
problem in your life to disappear or to

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.080
take one [music] action. And I think

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.240
food is one example of this. There's

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.600
actually wild statistics, look at this,

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.520
where they found the average American

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.578
household spent $3,933

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.760
on food away from home in 2023.

00:05:04.578 --> 00:05:07.120
>> [music]

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:09.400
>> Those numbers are old. I would guess it

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.080
is double that now. That's that's an

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.160
emergency fund for most people and we

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.120
spend it on Uber Eats. But it's not

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.320
really about the food, it's about every

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.200
moment you pay the premium [music] to

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.160
skip a little effort. And and I know

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.880
that seems normal now, each one feels

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.720
small, but stacked up, they're a second

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:27.880
rent payment you didn't even realize you

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:30.280
have. And the psychology is kind of

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.120
nasty on this. Default options will

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.400
shape your behavior because they

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.200
basically remove effort from one path

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.680
and add it to the other. So, these two

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.640
guys, Johnson and Goldstein, showed this

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.520
in an organ donation system, which is

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.280
kind of kind of a way to take this all

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.320
away, where default enrollment changed

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.160
participation rates dramatically across

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.680
countries. [music] So, if anything

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.280
becomes a default, we don't even

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.560
question it, which is a little silly

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.200
because yes, your organs save lives, but

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.480
they also make hospitals a lot of money.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.440
So, why are you not getting some of

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.640
that? Money works the same way in your

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.880
daily life. If the convenient option is

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:06.600
the easy default, and the cheaper option

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:08.000
requires planning, decisions, willpower,

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.880
then convenience is going to beat you up

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.880
every time. And so, I like to remember

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:15.080
that this system is designed [music] to

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.280
keep you comfy cozy, not rich. I'm not

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.720
going to tell you guys how to run your

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.720
life and what to eat [music] and that

